<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <Title>Quartz.NET MCP Server</Title>
    <Description>Quartz.NET MCP server - an endpoint that lets an AI agent read and operate the schedulers of a process and of its fleet over the Model Context Protocol, under the operator roles; $(Description)</Description>
    <PackageTags>$(PackageTags);mcp;model-context-protocol;ai;agent;aspnetcore</PackageTags>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Quartz</RootNamespace>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>True</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <!--
      The claim, and what it is worth - the same statement Quartz.AspNetCore.csproj makes, and read the same
      way. IsAotCompatible turns on IsTrimmable and the trim, AOT and single-file analyzers; what it adds is
      the statement, so the four are spelled out beside it.

      What is proven: Quartz.Trimming.Canary.Wire references this package, is published by ILCompiler as a
      native executable on every pull request, and is run. It maps the MCP endpoint on Kestrel and calls
      tools/list, list_jobs and pause_trigger by raw JSON-RPC, then previews a reschedule and confirms it with
      the Data Protection token the preview answered, every argument, answer and token payload read and written
      through the generated metadata of McpJsonContext, with reflection-based System.Text.Json switched off.

      Why it holds: the SDK's attribute tools and AIFunctionFactory reflect over a method's parameters, so
      no tool here is one. Each is an McpServerTool subclass whose input schema JsonSchemaExporter writes
      from a source-generated JsonTypeInfo, and whose arguments are read with the same metadata. The SDK
      says IsAotCompatible for itself.
    -->
    <IsAotCompatible>true</IsAotCompatible>
    <IsTrimmable>true</IsTrimmable>
    <EnableTrimAnalyzer>true</EnableTrimAnalyzer>
    <EnableAotAnalyzer>true</EnableAotAnalyzer>
    <EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>true</EnableSingleFileAnalyzer>
    <!--
      Off for the reason Quartz.Dashboard.Agent gives. ModelContextProtocol.AspNetCore 2.3.1 asks for
      10.0.13 of Microsoft.Extensions.Caching, Hosting and Logging abstractions, and pinning them at
      Quartz's 10.0.0 floors is NU1109. Off, the nuspec says Quartz.AspNetCore and the SDK, and a consumer
      inherits Quartz's floors through Quartz.
    -->
    <CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>false</CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
    <PackageReference Include="ModelContextProtocol.AspNetCore" />
    <ProjectReference Include="..\Quartz.AspNetCore\Quartz.AspNetCore.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
